teger point (2,b). For x=3, y = -1 + 7/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333, not integer. So only covers (1,2) and (4,1). So L_a covers 2 points.\n\nAlternatively, line through (4,1) and (2,2): slope (2-1)/(2-4) = 1/(-2) = -1/2. Equation: y - 1 = -1/2 (x - 4) => y = -1/2 x + 2 + 1 = -1/2 x + 3. For x=1, y = -0.5 + 3 = 2.5, not integer. For x=3, y = -1.5 + 3 = 1.5, not integer. So only (2,2) and (4,1). So L_b covers (2,2),(4,1). That's 2 points.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (3,2): slope (2-1)/(3-4) = 1/(-1) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (3,1): slope 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (2,1): slope (1-1)/(2-4) = 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (1,1): slope (1-1)/(1-4) = 0, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (1,3): slope (3-1)/(1-4) = 2/(-3) = -2/3, allowed. Equation: y - 1 = -2/3 (x - 4) => y = -2/3 x + 8/3 + 1 = -2/3 x + 11/3. For x=2, y = -4/3 + 11/3 = 7/3 ≈ 2.333, not integer. For x=3, y = -2 + 11/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.667. So only (1,3) and (4,1). So L_c covers (1,3),(4,1). That's 2 points.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (1,4): slope (4-1)/(1-4) = 3/(-3) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (2,3): slope (3-1)/(2-4) = 2/(-2) = -1, not allowed.\n\nLine through (4,1) and (3,1): slope 0, not allowed.\n\nThus any line through (4,1) can at most cover one other point (maybe none). So we need to allocate (4,1) to a line that also covers maybe one other point.\n\nNow column 3 has points (3,1),(3,2). We need to cover both. They cannot be covered by the same sunny line because that would require a line passing through both (3,1) and (3,2), which is vertical (x=3), not allowed. So they must be covered by different lines. So we need at least two lines to cover (3,1) and (3,2). Similarly, column 2 has points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3). We need to cover three points. Since each line can cover at most one point in column 2, we need at least three lines to cover them. But we have only 4 lines total. So we need to allocate lines such that each line covers at most one point in column 2. 